ael Granlund faceoff won against Jason Dickinson","type":{"id":"502","text":"Face Off","abbreviation":"faceoff"},"clock":{"displayValue":"1:06"},"period":{"number":1,"displayValue":"1st"},"team":{"id":"25"},"participants":[{"athlete":{"id":"5831","displayName":"Mikael Granlund","shortName":"M. Granlund"},"type":"face-off-winner"},{"athlete":{"id":"3042062","displayName":"Jason Dickinson","shortName":"J. Dickinson"}}]},{"id":"401892456000000890","scoringPlay":false,"scoreValue":0,"awayScore":0,"homeScore":0,"text":"Trent Frederic hit Pavel Mintyukov","type":{"id":"503","text":"Hit","abbreviation":"hit"},"clock":{"displayValue":"1:10"},"period":{"number":1,"displayValue":"1st"},"team":{"id":"6"},"participants":[{"athlete":{"id":"4024997","displayName":"Trent Frederic","shortName":"T. Frederic"},"type":"hit-credited"},{"athlete":{"id":"5080154","displayName":"Pavel Mintyukov","shortName":"P. Mintyukov"},"type":"hitted"}]}],"gameInfo":{"venue":{"id":"1842","fullName":"Honda Center","address":{"city":"Anaheim","state":"CA","zipCode":"92899","country":"USA"},"grass":false,"images":[{"href":"https://a.espncdn.com/i/venues/nhl/day/1842.jpg","width":2000,"height":1125,"alt":"","rel":["full","day"]}]},"attendance":17174,"officials":[{"fullName":"Ryan Gibbons","displayName":"Ryan Gibbons","position":{"name":"Linesman","displayName":"Linesman","id":"25"},"order":0},{"fullName":"Trevor Hanson","displayName":"Trevor Hanson","position":{"name":"Referee","displayName":"Referee","id":"24"},"order":0},{"fullName":"Tom Chmielewski","displayName":"Tom Chmielewski","position":{"name":"Referee","displayName":"Referee","id":"24"},"order":0},{"fullName":"James Tobias","displayName":"James Tobias","position":{"name":"Linesman","displayName":"Linesman","id":"25"},"order":0}]},"article":{"headline":"Evan Bouchard continues historic start, Oilers pummel Ducks in 2nd period in 5-2 win","description":"— Evan Bouchard had two goals and an assist to become the second defenseman in NHL history with at least six goals through the first four games of a season, and the Edmonton Oilers used a five-goal second period to beat the Anaheim Ducks 5-2 on...","story":"ANAHEIM, Calif. -- — <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/4352722/evan-bouchard\">Evan Bouchard</a> had two goals and an assist to become the second defenseman in NHL history with at least six goals through the first four games of a season, and the <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/team/_/name/edm/edmonton-oilers\">Edmonton Oilers</a> used a five-goal second period to beat the <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/team/_/name/ana/anaheim-ducks\">Anaheim Ducks</a> 5-2 on Wednesday night.\n\n\r\n              Bouchard has a league-leading six goals through four games. Harry Cameron had eight goals in four games for the Toronto Arenas during the NHL's inaugural season in 1917-18.\n\n\r\n              <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/3114727/leon-draisaitl\">Leon Draisaitl</a> had four assists, <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/3895074/connor-mcdavid\">Connor McDavid</a> had a goal and two assists, <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/3114775/kasperi-kapanen\">Kasperi Kapanen</a> and <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/4565231/vasily-podkolzin\">Vasily Podkolzin</a> also scored, and the Oilers overcame a sluggish start to win their third straight game. <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/4894487/devon-levi\">Devon Levi</a> made 28 saves.\n\n\r\n              <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/5216858/beckett-sennecke\">Beckett Sennecke</a> had two power-play goals and <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/5149153/leo-carlsson\">Leo Carlsson</a> had two assists, but the Ducks lost for the first time in three games. <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/4588165/lukas-dostal\">Lukas Dostal</a> made 12 saves, stopping just six of 11 shots in the decisive middle period before being replaced to start the third. <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/3151137/ville-husso\">Ville Husso</a> made two saves in relief.\n\n\r\n              Bouchard scored twice in the second period, sparking Edmonton’s rally by cutting it to 2-1 11 seconds into the frame on a long wrist shot through traffic. He put the Oilers in front 3-2 on a one-timer from Draisaitl with 5:48 to go.\n\n\r\n              Bouchard is the fifth defenseman in league history with at least 10 points through the first four games of a season. The feat was last accomplished by <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/2976833/morgan-rielly\">Morgan Rielly</a> in 2018-19 for the Maple Leafs.\n\n\r\n              McDavid capped off the five-goal outburst by netting his third goal and 12th point of the season with 51.7 left during 4-on-4 play.\n\n\r\n              After scoring one power-play goal in 82 games as a rookie, Sennecke found the back of the net twice on the man-advantage in the first period.\n\n\r\n              Ducks winger <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/5361658/nikita-klepov\">Nikita Klepov</a>, the No. 15 pick in the 2026 draft, made his NHL debut and became the youngest player in franchise history at 18 years, 102 days old.\n\n\r\n              <hl2>Up next</hl2>\r\n              Oilers: At San Jose on Saturday.\n\n\r\n              Kings: At Winnipeg on Friday night.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                <a href=\"https://apnews.com/hub/nhl\">See AP’s full NHL coverage here</a>","published":"2026-10-08T08:05:46Z"}}